for as long as I can remember or at  least since I was four years old I  wanted to start a company by the age of  eight I had launched my first somewhat  successful venture which involved  reselling my mom's Evian bottles from  her fridge to construction workers in  our neighborhood she purchased these at  around a dollar fifty a bottle and I  resold them for fifty cents and they  sold out great but this was an early  lesson that even when your mom is your  investor it's important to generate a  positive return on capital for the  business to be sustainable fast-forward  20 years and I got that call the call  that we all know well that I had gotten  into Stanford Graduate School Business  it feels a lot like winning the lottery  we all know the stats less than seven  percent acceptance rate which means that  a lot of very qualified people who  deserve to be here don't get the chance  to so I felt pretty blessed and I was  very excited to be starting my  entrepreneurial journey that is until a  few weeks later I came across another  statistic that somewhere between one and  five percent of venture capital funding  goes to women entrepreneurs said another  way it is more likely to get into  Stanford Business School than it is to  be a woman entrepreneur who secures  venture capital funding I found this to  be shocking and as someone who came from  a background and investing and  fundraising I thought this is a great  business idea it must be there must be a  market deficiency that I could solve  through a company it's even the case  with the popular show shark tank women  who pips themselves for shark tank are  less likely to receive capital and when  they do receive capital they're more  likely to be priced down by the Sharks  than their male counterparts so back to  my business if there's anything I've  learned as an investor it's that being  right on the wrong time frame is the  same thing as being wrong and turns out  i was a little bit late to the game  there's actually plenty of companies out  there that are doing this today serving  women entrepreneurs their alternative  capital markets through accelerators  through networks of professional women  but you don't hear about this in the  narrative today and so I'm asking myself  why when there are so many opportunities  for women entrepreneurs is the only  thing that you hear about is how  difficult it is I'd like to propose that  we need to reframe the discussion  because actually there's never been a  better time to be a woman entrepreneur  some private equity or some venture  capital firms do recognize this  opportunity first round capital in a  study of its ten years of operating  history found that women led ventures  outperform their mail only firms by  sixty-three percent but whether you're a  man or a woman as an entrepreneur it's  important to recognize that venture  capital isn't one size fits all it's not  the right solution for every business  questions that you should ask yourself  are how important is it to me to retain  control of my company how quickly do I  want to grow this business if you decide  that venture capital is right for you  it's also important to note that not all  venture firms are created equally some  specialized by industry others focus on  later stage versus early-stage gsv  alumna jessica heron who's the founder  of stella and dot recognized the  importance of finding the right capital  for her business she shopped around her  idea for a direct-to-consumer jewelry  business too many different investors  before finding the right one who believe  not only in her business model but also  the vision that she had for how quickly  she was going to get there it was  important to her to prioritize not only  her business but also her family and  arguably her wildly successful business  is well positioned today because of the  time that she took to find the right  capital provider outside of venture  capital there are so many resources  today in the form of crowdfunding and  equity funding and the great news is  that women are disproportionately  successful in these channels Kickstarter  reports that women led tech businesses  are more than twice as likely to be  successful in raising campaigns than men  another GSB alumna nejo Woodward is  using Kickstarter to prove early-stage  product market fit and gain customer  traction she's designing a line of dolls  and stories that are inspiring the next  generation of girls that they can be  women entrepreneurs or coders  journalists or politicians and she's  doing so through Kickstarter so the way  that Kickstarter and crowdfunding works  is that early contributors give capital  in exchange for goods or services  outside of traditional crowdfunding  there is equity crowdfunding in which  entrepreneurs can actually raise real  equity and women are also really kicking  ass in this channel as well Circle up  which is one of the leading firms in the  space reports that women are are more  than five times the successful raising  capital online then they are through VC  and more than nine times more likely to  raise capital online than they are  through traditional banks one woman  entrepreneur who raised capital through  circle app suggests that raising capital  online takes gender completely out of  the equation because most of the  communication is written  I'm optimistic about the future for  women entrepreneurs particularly because  some suggest that alternative capital  markets like circle up we're going to  outstrip venture capital within the next  ten years but as optimistic as I am  about the future it's important to  reflect back on where we've been i was  born in january 1988 into a world in  which women couldn't secure a business  loan without a male cosigner within my  lifetime women gained access to this  fundamental source of business financing  that's pretty staggering to me and yet  women have been creating businesses for  generations and successfully doing it  one example that's close to my heart is  my great grandmother Myrtle she started  a successful fine fashion business in  muncie indiana of all places in 1956  when most women were simply housewives  and she did so while raising two kids  she and the other women who've come  before me are my inspiration I hope that  this is just the beginning of the  conversation and that by sharing stories  of success we can start to change the  pattern because venture capital is all  about pattern recognition the more  successful women entrepreneurs that do  well with institutional capital they're  more likely investors will be to follow  the herd as an aspiring woman  entrepreneur myself I'm throwing my hat  in the ring will you join me thank you  you
 

